    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Bartter’s and Gitelman’s syndromes are a family of inherited autosomal recessive disorders in which genetic mutations inhibit diuretic-sensitive sodium transporters and result in hypotension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. We identified a patient who exhibits a mixed phenotype between Bartter’s and Gitelman’s syndrome and has progressive chronic kidney disease. We employed a candidate gene approach to identify mutations in the CLCNKB gene, a gene often considered in patients with a mixed phenotype of this nature, in an index patient of Filipino descent. We then sought to link the mutation to disruption of ClC-Kb protein function by characterizing the mutant phenotype using in vitro studies. We identified a novel gene mutation (nt G499T, aa G167C) at a conserved site within the ion permeation pathway of the channel which, based on structural-function studies in the lower order Chloride channel family member, CLC-0, switches the ion selectivity of the channel from chloride to sodium. We assessed for and found significant differences in levels of gene expression and cell surface protein expression between the wild type and mutant G167C in cultured cells. Furthermore, we found that the mutant G167C was localized on the basolateral membrane of cells in the thick ascending limb and distal convoluted tubules from biopsies of affected family members. Based on these studies, we have described a novel mutation in the pore of ClC-Kb, and suggest that the G167C mutation contributes to the atypical presentation of Bartter-Gitelman’s syndrome, possibly leading to progressive chronic kidney disease.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
